(a) This section shall be applicable to chapter 6, animals, and all articles, divisions, sections, and subsections therein.
(b) As used in this section, the term "nuisance" is defined to include any animal that:
(1) Molests passersby or passing vehicles;
(2) Attacks other animals;
(3) Trespasses, goes upon, or is found on school grounds, public grounds, private grounds or property, or grounds or property of another without the permission of the owner of said property;
(4) Is repeatedly at large (repeatedly shall mean five times or more);
(5) Damages private or public property;
(6) Barks, whines, moans, hisses, snarls, shrieks, yowls, caterwauls, screams, or howls in an excessive, continuous, or untimely fashion;
(7) Urinates, defecates, marks with scent, secretes any material, or otherwise causes any odors or any of the foregoing to be upon the property of another; or
(8) Nuisance shall also include any violation(s) by and animal or owner thereof of any of subsections (c) through (h) herein, or any parts thereof.
(c) Every female dog, cat, or other animal in heat shall be kept confined in a building or secure enclosure or in a veterinary clinic or hospital or in a kennel in such a manner that such female dog, cat, or other animal cannot come into contact with another dog, cat, or other animal of like kind, except for intentional breeding purposes.
(d) An owner of a domesticated animal, whether vaccinated or not vaccinated, shall confine such domesticated animal within an adequate fence or enclosure or within a house, garage, or other building to prevent the animal from running at large. No animal shall be tied or tethered as a permanent measure of restraint. No animal shall be left unattended on a chain, lead, runner, cable, rope, leash, or similar tethering device.
(e) An owner of a dog, cat, or other animal, whether vaccinated or not vaccinated, shall prevent such dog, cat, or other animal from becoming a danger to persons or property at any location, or trespassing upon another persons' property without that person's permission.
(f) An owner of a dog, cat, or other animal, whether vaccinated or not vaccinated, shall prevent such dog, cat, or other animal from running at large upon private property, or the streets, sidewalks, alleys, parks, or other public places in the city of Byron.
(g) Upon a finding by the municipal judge that any animal constitutes a public nuisance within the definition of this section, the judge may order the owner or custodian to abate the nuisance by permanently removing the animal from the city limits. If the owner or custodian fails to abide by such order, the municipal judge, after a hearing, may order the animal to be destroyed by the appropriate city authority.
(h) In addition to any and all other penalties provided for in this Code, or by any court, any and all violation(s) hereof are subject to the provisions, penalties, fines, and sanctions as found in section 1-8 of this Code.
(Ord. No. 2023-7, 9-11-2023)

